    "lens": {
      "mechanics": "When spending financed by money and credit grows faster than the amount sold, prices rise. Gold pays no interest, so higher after-inflation bond yields increase the return an investor gives up by holding it.",
      "data": "Three forces carry into Monday. First, <a href=\"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/archives/ppi_11132026.htm\">October producer prices rose 0.3% on the month</a>, down from a revised 1.4% in September in the 13 Nov report. Second, <a href=\"https://home.treasury.gov/resource-center/data-chart-center/interest-rates/TextView?type=daily_treasury_real_yield_curve&amp;field_tdr_date_value_month=202611\">Friday’s 10-year real yield was 2.91%</a>, giving bonds a larger return advantage over gold. Third, the <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/T10YIE\">10-year inflation compensation gauge held at 2.24% on Friday</a>. Real yields can fall when inflation expectations rise faster than bond yields. On Friday the real yield rose and market inflation compensation held steady. Cash Treasury levels still reflect Friday.",
